                <h2>Scenario: The Numbers Don't Add Up at Month-End Reconciliation</h2>
<p>Lao Zhou, the performance marketing lead at a utility app team, recently encountered a headache-inducing problem.</p>
<p>At the end of November, the finance team discovered a discrepancy: three platforms (Meta, TikTok, Google) had cumulative recharges of $286,000, but the backend showed actual spend of $273,000, while invoices totaled only $268,000. With nearly $20,000 unaccounted for, finance was pushing for answers and the agency couldn't explain it clearly.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. Among the overseas teams we've worked with, at least 60% have encountered similar "recharge-spend-invoice" triangular accounting problems. Today, we'll break this issue down clearly.</p>
<h2>Problem Analysis: Why Three Numbers Don't Match</h2>
<h3>1. Recharge Amount ≠ Actual Spend</h3>
<p>Advertising platform recharge logic works differently than you might expect. When you recharge $1,000, the backend may show an account balance of $1,000, but actual spend is affected by:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Timezone differences</strong>: Some platforms charge based on UTC time, creating 1-2 day misalignment with your financial cycle</li>
<li><strong>Refunds and rebates</strong>: Spend rebates and account balance refunds don't immediately reflect in spend data</li>
<li><strong>Currency conversion losses</strong>: Recharge involves exchange rates, and actual received amounts may fluctuate due to rate changes</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Spend Amount ≠ Invoice Amount</h3>
<p>Invoices are the platform's final revenue confirmation and are often less than backend spend due to:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Invalid traffic deductions</strong>: Platforms like Meta conduct post-hoc deductions for fraudulent traffic, with spend data retroactively adjusted</li>
<li><strong>Tax point differences</strong>: Some regional invoices exclude taxes, or actual tax deductions don't match</li>
<li><strong>Credit offset</strong>: Some enterprise clients have payment terms, with invoice issuance lagging behind spend</li>
</ul>
<h2>Judgment Logic: Locate the Source First</h2>
<p>When numbers don't match, don't rush to assign blame. Investigate in this order:</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Verify recharge records against receipt confirmations</strong><br />
Confirm the date, amount, and account ID for each recharge. Pay special attention to agency transfer vs. direct recharge differences.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Pull platform backend spend reports</strong><br />
Export official reports and cross-reference with account backend balances. Distinguish between "campaign spend" and "account operation fees" (such as BM management fees).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Compare invoices with platform statements</strong><br />
Some platforms (like Google) issue final monthly statements between the 5th and 10th of the following month—this is the settlement basis.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Identify the nature of discrepancies</strong><br />
Differences are classified as "explainable" or "pending." Refunds, rebates, and exchange rate variances fall into the former; unreceived recharges and duplicate charges require recovery.</p>
<h2>Action Steps: Four Steps for Reconciliation Closure</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Build a Multi-Platform Reconciliation Ledger</h3>
<p>Use a master sheet to manage all accounts with key fields: recharge date, recharge amount, platform-confirmed receipt, spend amount, invoice amount, and variance.</p>
